===

title:FrontalLobe
tags: 
--- 
<<FrontalLobe YarnCommands>>
Let's begin with the blue region on the left called frontal lobe! It's the boss of decision-making, planning, and controlling voluntry movements. Its also where your personality lives.  #line:04a357a 
It also controls your voluntary movements through an area called the motor cortex.   #line:0cc0e23 
When you speak, you can thank a special part here called Broca’s area for helping you form words.   #line:0f54db3 
And, perhaps most importantly, the frontal lobe plays a big role in shaping your emotions, personality, and social behavior. #line:0a5040d 
Fun Fact , damage to this can completely change how a person acts or reacts to things. #line:06a3455 
You may press the button with the matching color to see the name of this lobe.  #line:09f401b 

//<<deFrontalLobe YarnCommands>>
<<jump ParietalLobe>>
===
title: ParietalLobe
tags:
---

<<PeritalLobe YarnCommands >>

The "parietal lobe" is like your brain's touch and taste center. It's the yellow structure at the top the frontal lobe. It is like your your brain's GPS, helping you understand where your body is in space and how to move it. #line:0bdb34d 
It processes touch sensations, like feeling a soft blanket or the warmth of the sun on your skin.  #line:0d07134 
It also helps you understand where your body is in space, so you can touch your nose with your eyes closed or catch a ball without looking.  #line:0e71c25 


Lets keeps going to the next lobe! #line:0f30b18 
//<<dePeritalLobe YarnCommands>>
<<jump temporallobe>>


===
title: temporallobe
tags:
---

<<TemporalLobe YarnCommands>>

The green structure on the left side is the "temporal lobe" is like your brain's music and memory center. It helps your understand language an remember stuff. #line:0478305 

It's also where you recognize faces and understand what people are saying. #line:075827f 

//<<deTemporalLobe YarnCommands>>
<<jump occipitallobe >>


===
title: occipitallobe
tags:
---

<<OccipitalLobe YarnCommands>>

The "occipital lobe" is at the back of the brain in red which is like your brain's movie screen. It processes everything you see, from colors to shapes to movement. #line:05ad95d 

When you look at a beautiful sunset or read a book, it’s the occipital lobe that makes sense of those images. #line:0d5e799 

It’s also where your brain puts together all the visual information it gets from your eyes, so you can see the world clearly. #line:0274d95 

//<<deOccipitalLobe YarnCommands>>
<<jump prefrontal>>


===
title: prefrontal
tags:
---
<<PrefrontalLobe YarnCommands>>

Next, let's zoom in on the very front tip of the frontal lobe. It is the green structure at the bottom .  #line:0f06808 

Think of it as the CEO's desk inside the brian's head office. This is where the big, complex thinking happens. #line:06b7d25 

It helps you plan for the future, make decisions and weighs pros and cons. It also acts like a brake pedal, helping you control impuses so you dont just do the first thing that pops into your head.  #line:0a08cf8 

When you're remembering something just long enough to use it-like a phone number before you dial-it's the prefrontal cortex that helps you keep that information in mind. #line:075aecc 

It even shapes your social behavior and personality, guiding how you interact with people and read social cues. #line:0904ae5 

Fun fact, this part of the brian isnt fully development until your mid twenties, which is why teenagers sometimes make impulsive decisions. #line:0432e92 

//<<dePrefrontalLobe YarnCommands>>
<<jump internal >>



===
title: internal
tags:   
---

Now that you know about different lobes, let's take a closer look at the internal structures of the brain. #line:013cd3b 

If you have still not seperated the lobes, pinch over them and seperate it. Realse the pinch at different location once you have the internal part visible. #line:0f8b1c2

Once you’ve opened up the brain, press the buttons to discover each part. #line:0eb4d97 

Lets start with Cerebellum. #line:0a212de 
<<jump cerebellum>>


===
title: cerebellum
tags:
---
<<Cerebellum YarnCommands>>
The cerebellum is like the brain's balance and coordination center. It helps you keep your balance when you walk, ride a bike, or dance. #line:03807f8 

It’s also responsible for fine motor skills, like writing or playing an instrument. When you learn a new skill, like riding a bike, the cerebellum helps you practice until it becomes second nature. #line:0be89c7 

Without it, walking or even holding a cup steady would be tough! #line:0773ac5 

Lets move on to the next part, the brainstem. #line:08905e7 
//<<deCerebellum YarnCommands>>
<<jump brainstem>>


===
title: brainstem
tags:
---
<<BrainStem YarnCommands>>

Now, look at the brainstem — that stalk-like structure right at the center bottom, under the brain. #line:0012915 

It’s the vital connection between your brain and spinal cord, managing life-essential functions like breathing and heartbeat. It’s a true life-support hub. #line:0e13ba8 

The brainstem is like the brain's autopilot. It controls all the automatic functions that keep you alive, like breathing, heart rate, and blood pressure. #line:088011e 

It’s also responsible for basic reflexes, like blinking when something comes close to your eyes or pulling your hand away from something hot. #line:0769a00 

lets jump over to the thalamus. #line:0c3affc 
//<<deBrainStem YarnCommands>>

<<jump thalamus>>


===
title: thalamus
tags:
---

<<Thalamus YarnCommands>>

Here’s the thalamus and the surrounding midbrain structures, the central bulge just above the brainstem. #line:001bed5 

Think of it as the brain’s relay station — it channels sensory information to the cerebral cortex so you can make sense of the world. It’s like a traffic cop, directing signals from your senses to the right parts of the brain. #line:06c7b76 

Hope you now have a better understanding of the brain's  structures! Let's wrap up our journey through the brain. #line:0d5e79a
//<<deThalamus YarnCommands>>
<<jump buttons>>


===

